# Primary sources checked for C003-S001

Access date: 2026-10-26. This is a targeted source review, not a novelty search.
No priority, unpublishedness, or general identification claim is made.

1. **S1: Pascal Baumann, Moses Ganardi, Ramanathan S. Thinniyam, Georg Zetzsche.**
   *Existential Definability over the Subword Ordering*, STACS 2022, LIPIcs 219,
   article 7, DOI 10.4230/LIPIcs.STACS.2022.7.
   [Primary paper](https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol219-stacs2022/LIPIcs.STACS.2022.7/LIPIcs.STACS.2022.7.pdf).
   Checked Lemma 3.11, printed page 7:10: all binary erasing projections determine
   a word when the alphabet has at least two symbols. Our singleton-block case
   uses equal occurrence counts separately. Repetition is allowed by that lemma.
   The journal expansion renumbers the analogous statement Lemma 3.12, so this
   record deliberately cites the STACS version/number. We apply known word
   reconstruction; we did not invent it.

2. **S2: Jean Berstel and Luc Boasson.** *Shuffle factorization is unique*,
   Theoretical Computer Science 273 (2002), 47–67,
   DOI 10.1016/S0304-3975(00)00433-3.
   [Author-hosted primary paper](https://www-igm.univ-mlv.fr/~berstel/Articles/2002ShuffleFactorizationUnique.pdf).
   Checked abstract and opening problem statement: factorization of an entire
   finite shuffle set into a normalized multiset of words, with unary
   normalization caveats. This concerns equality to the whole finite language,
   not recovery of an alphabet partition from a partial positive/negative sample.
   We do not transfer its whole-language uniqueness theorem to our passive data.

3. **S3: Franziska Biegler, Mark Daley and Ian McQuillan.** *Algorithmic
   decomposition of shuffle on words*, Theoretical Computer Science 454 (2012),
   38–50, DOI 10.1016/j.tcs.2012.04.001.
   [Publisher primary abstract](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0304397512003234).
   Checked metadata/abstract only: under conditions on an input DFA it constructs
   candidate words if a two-word shuffle decomposition exists; candidate recovery
   is distinguished from verifying decomposability. Our input is finite labeled
   observations and an alphabet, with the stronger disjoint-ownership assumption,
   rather than a complete DFA language. No claim to subsume its algorithm.

4. **S4: Henry, Mousavi, Neele, Sammartino**, *A Detailed Account of Compositional
   Automata Learning through Alphabet Refinement*, arXiv:2504.16624v3.
   [Primary text](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16624v3).
   Consulted model/Proposition 5/Corollary 12 for relation to the earlier general
   projection observation criterion. This is related compositional learning, not
   a source asserting our proposed positive-opposition certificate is novel.

The connected bipartite proof and uv/vu coverage construction are given in full
in PROOFS.md so they can be assessed independently of source attribution.
Opposition here means observed positive pair variation under singleton factors;
it must not be confused with a learned independence relation for arbitrary
regular factors or with the dependence graph of a trace monoid.

Searches included: word binary projections Lemma 3.11; shuffle learning singleton
words alphabet decomposition; shuffle decomposition disjoint alphabets; word
projections commutation reconstruction; Algorithmic decomposition of shuffle on
words; Shuffle factorization is unique. Irrelevant ML shuffle/blog results and
secondary summaries were not used as evidence. The DOI open for
10.1016/j.ic.2017.09.002 returned an internal accessibility error; a primary search
snippet exposed related references, but that inaccessible page is not an adopted
proof source. No unavailable source text was reconstructed.
